A||N||16||87ECO5||Coating factory springs with rubber?||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||10-18-2001||03:45 PM||||I've got a friend that has recently installed coilovers on his SE.  If I'm not mistaken, the springs are coated with a rubberish material that's blue in color.<P>Is there a place that can coat springs?  I'm about to install stiffer factory springs on my economy coupe and I'd like to have this same type of coating on them.<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||10-18-2001||07:00 PM||||Scott, just wrap 'em in duct tape and paint 'em with a magic marker, no one will notice.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black86SE||10-18-2001||07:08 PM||||Why paint'em, just get colored duct tape. They also have camoflauge duct tape. Then nobody would know they were there and think you were just floating. oooooh. <p>[This message has been edited by Black86SE (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||10-18-2001||08:07 PM||||Rust creates stress risers on the surface of the coils and eventually will cause the coil to break when stressed.  Rubber coating is less likely to crack and peel off like paint and is superb for preventing rust.  That's the reason they're not just painted or wrapped in duct tape from the factory.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Where to have them dipped near you, I don't know, sorry.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChopTop||10-18-2001||08:13 PM||||You might want to try Eastwood.  They do sell a product that you dip your tool handles in for rubber coating.  Doubt you could use it on your springs but they may have an idea since they specialize in restoration supplies.||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||10-18-2001||10:45 PM||||oooohhhh, I liked the camoflauge idea!! Or, you could wrap them in used wine bottle labels for the "decorative" look......or, maybe the girls could color them with their crayons........or, maybe......oh, never mind...<P>(Tigger, I was just kidding Scott about the duct tape thing...)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||10-18-2001||10:53 PM||||Hehe.<P>I forgot about the stuff from Eastwood, good idea!||||reg||3
Z||000007||Rick 88||10-18-2001||10:59 PM||||How about using black rubberized undercoating that comes in a spray can. It will flex and not be tacky when dry. <P>Rick 88||||reg||3
Z||000008||Juiced_V8||10-18-2001||11:04 PM||||the process is called powder coating and almost any sandblasting shop can tell you where to get it done <BR>it expensive but its what some cars come with stock  and its available in bunches o colors<P>------------------<BR>86 gt v8 fogger set up sold going to get a simple sniper system with a plate||||reg||12
